# Auth / OTP invariants

## Never trap the user
Any blocking auth screen (verify, paywall "checking…") MUST keep an escape hatch
that signs out and returns to `/` (e.g. the verify "Back" button calling
`logoutThenClearQueryCache`). Don't remove it or hide it behind a stalled state.

## Rate limiting is server-authoritative
The client countdown is cosmetic; the server enforces the real limit. Never relax a
server limit to "fix" a client UX issue.

- `resend-verification`: per-email escalating cooldown (`lib/resendCooldown.ts`,
  60s→120s→300s→600s) **plus** a per-IP cap. The per-email 429 returns `waitSecs`;
  the per-IP 429 must NOT (so the device doesn't start a long countdown on a shared-IP
  false positive).
- `register`: keep the per-IP cap (`registerHitByIp`) — it blocks email-bombing via
  repeated sign-ups regardless of which email is targeted.
- `verify-email`: keep both the per-IP request/wrong-code limits and the per-email
  attempt block.

## Cooldown steps must stay in sync
`mobile/lib/resendCooldown.ts` (`RESEND_COOLDOWN_STEPS_SECS`) mirrors
`artifacts/api-server/src/lib/resendCooldown.ts` (`RESEND_COOLDOWN_STEPS_MS`). If you
change one, change the other and update both test files.

## Client persists the deadline
The resend cooldown is persisted (AsyncStorage, keyed by email) so leaving/returning
or relaunching cannot reset it. On a `waitSecs` 429, resume the countdown from the
server value. Don't store the limit only in component state.
